Spinal Cord Compression
Palliative & End of Life
Localized severe back pain, Ascending lower limb weakness, Distinct sensory level

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-047
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of profound leg weakness, inability to walk, and excruciating back pain.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed the rapid onset of bilateral lower limb weakness progressing over the last 6 hours, accompanied by severe, localized back pain and an inability to pass urine. This presentation is a classical oncological emergency. Urgent, immediate after-hours assessment was absolutely mandatory to perform a focused neurological exam, administer high-dose corticosteroids, and arrange emergency hospital transfer to prevent permanent, irreversible paraplegia and autonomic failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My back is in absolute agony, and my legs feel like heavy logs. I tried to stand up to go to the toilet, but I completely collapsed, and I haven't been able to pee all day."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 48 hours, the patient has experienced progressive, severe, and localized back pain strictly exacerbating when lying flat or coughing. Today, this rapidly evolved into radicular pain distinctly shooting bilaterally down the posterior thighs. Over the last 6 hours, there has been ascending, symmetrical lower limb weakness explicitly progressing rapidly towards complete paraplegia. The patient reports severe numbness extending from the toes up to the umbilicus. There is sudden onset of absolute urinary retention strictly accompanied by a highly distended, painless bladder. The daughter notes the patient experienced profound mechanical instability and a near-fall immediately prior to the complete paralysis setting in this evening.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound and paralyzed from the waist down.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending permanent paraplegia; extremely high risk of pressure injuries and irreversible bowel/bladder dysfunction if not decompressed immediately.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/90 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is in severe pain, lying rigidly flat, terrified, and completely unable to move their lower extremities.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals Grade 1/5 power globally in both lower limbs. There is hyperreflexia at the knees and ankles, with a distinct, sustained bilateral upgoing plantar response (Babinski sign) indicating severe upper motor neuron disease. Sensory examination reveals a distinct, sharply defined sensory level explicitly mapped to the T10 dermatome (umbilicus). Severe saddle anesthesia is present. Abdominal exam reveals a visibly distended, palpable bladder extending to the umbilicus. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the localized severe back pain (thoracolumbar junction), ascending lower limb weakness, and the distinct sensory level at T10.
Environmental Observations: N/A - Emergency transfer required.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral analgesics are completely failing to control the severe mechanical/neuropathic pain of the compression.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Spinal Cord Compression - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Oncological Emergency)
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Unlikely to be purely lower motor neuron due to the presence of hyperreflexia and upgoing plantars, indicating true cord compression higher up (T10), though the saddle anesthesia and retention overlap.

Acute transverse myelitis - Ruled out/unlikely given the known history of malignancy and the classic preceding progressive, mechanical bone pain before the neurological collapse.

Aortic dissection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of "tearing" chest pain, equal upper limb pulses, and the distinct ascending symmetrical neurological march.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This is a time-critical, catastrophic oncological emergency. The objective neurological findings—dense paraparesis, a distinct T10 sensory level, upper motor neuron signs, and acute urinary retention—definitively confirm an acute malignant spinal cord compression. The window to salvage independent ambulation and sphincter control is measured in hours. Delaying assessment or attempting to manage this purely with analgesia in the community until morning would guarantee irreversible, permanent paraplegia, destroying the patient's remaining quality of life. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to diagnose the compression, administer stat high-dose dexamethasone to reduce cord edema, and orchestrate an immediate blue-light ambulance transfer for urgent MRI and neurosurgical/radiation oncology intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat intravenous Dexamethasone (16mg) immediately to rapidly decrease the peritumoral edema compressing the spinal cord. Administered stat subcutaneous morphine for the agonizing back pain. Inserted an indwelling urinary catheter to relieve the acute, massive retention (drained 900ml). Arranged an immediate Priority 1 ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary hospital.
Positive Behavioral Change: When being moved by the paramedics (trigger), explicitly cross your arms over your chest and keep your spine completely rigid (behavior). This strictly minimizes any twisting or flexing of the spine, directly preventing the tumor from shearing further into the spinal cord and causing irreversible damage (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Immediate hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Neurosurgical and radiation oncology review immediately upon ED arrival.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance has been dispatched. If the ambulance is delayed and the patient loses consciousness, or the pain becomes entirely unmanageable despite the morphine, the daughter must call 000 to update the priority.

